# build artifacts
_build/

# vendored registry dependencies materialised by `moon update` / `moon add`, and
# by `moonx <target>` when the target is a registry coordinate (it runs
# `moon fetch`, which drops the package under `.repos/`).
# NOT ours to commit: it is a copy of someone else's published source, and
# `git add -A` will happily stage thousands of files of it. (Learned the hard
# way — a release commit once swept in the whole of moonbitlang/async.)
.mooncakes/
.repos/

# local formal-verification toolchain shims (machine-specific paths)
.why3.conf
cvc5wrap.ps1

# scratch logs from measurement runs (scan output dumps, big-tree timings).
# Evidence, not source: the numbers quoted in the docs are already recorded in
# EXTENSIONS.md, and these files are megabytes of per-file listing.
_qa/

# `_verify` scratch. THE RULE IS THE CONVENTION: a file whose name starts with
# an underscore is a SCRATCH FILE, and a scratch file is never tracked. It is
# the same rule `.moonignore` states for the package, and the same one the
# package gate checks ("the scratch probes and working copies", `^_verify\_`).
#
# 0643 MEASURED WHAT THE CONVENTION COST. Seventy files sat in this directory
# as UNTRACKED scratch, and six of them were doing real work -- a TRACKED file
# NAMED them, so a reader of the shipped documents was pointed at a file that
# no clean checkout carried. The fix was NOT an exception list: the same names
# would then have to be spelled in `.gitignore`, in `.moonignore` AND in the
# package gate -- a rule spelled three times is a rule that drifts. It was to
# PROMOTE them: a file that does work is RENAMED to drop the underscore and
# tracked, and every reference to it is updated.
#
# So: `_` = scratch = never tracked. A scratch file that starts doing work gets
# PROMOTED, not excepted. The one tracked underscore file is the provenance of
# `_verify/NARRATIVE.md` -- the NARRATIVE's own first line names it, so a
# reader has to be able to open it.
_verify/_*
!_verify/_split_release_narrative.ps1
# `remeasure.ps1` writes one full per-file scan per corpus into this directory,
# for the same reason and with the same size. It is NOT covered by the
# `_verify/_*.txt` rule above because that rule only matches files directly in
# `_verify`, not a subdirectory of it -- so `git add _verify` swept six
# megabyte dumps in before anyone noticed.
_verify/remeasure_out/
# Per-gate transcripts written by `Run-Gate` (release.ps1). One file per mutation
# gate, rewritten on every release run. Evidence, not source -- and it only exists
# BECAUSE the summary filter was hiding gate failures: a run that reports a gate
# FAILED now prints the path so the full text survives. Not covered by the
# `_verify/_*.txt` rule above for the same reason `remeasure_out` is not: `*` does
# not cross `/`, so a rule for files directly in `_verify` never matches a
# subdirectory of it. These are `.log` as well as nested, so both facts apply.
_verify/_gatelogs/

# The mooncakes credential. `git add -A` staged it once before .moonignore
# existed; the token inside is live. The copy CI needs is a GitHub Actions
# secret, not a file in the tree. .moonignore covers the publish side of this.
credentials.json
*.credentials.json

# Scratch commit messages. These are the release notes BEFORE the 0497 README
# restructure moved them into CHANGELOG.md; the copies at the repository root
# and under `_verify` are the ORIGINALS, and they are not source -- four of them
# were tracked, and four of them reached the registry in every published version
# up to and including 0.4.33 (MEASURED against the published 0.4.33 tree).
#
# The release record is CHANGELOG.md / CHANGELOG.zh.md, and the reasoning behind
# each increment is _verify/NARRATIVE.md. Both ship. .moonignore keeps these out
# of the package; these rules keep them out of the repository.
COMMIT_*.md
_verify/commitmsg*.txt
